---

### 2. `pyproject.toml` (Añadir extras y metadata de repositorio)
Agregamos la opción `all` y URLs de issues y documentación, lo que ayuda al bot de JOSS y a los revisores a ver una configuración empaquetada estándar:

```toml
[build-system]
requires = ["setuptools>=61.0"]
build-backend = "setuptools.build_meta"

[project]
name = "quang"
version = "0.2.0"
description = "The Qang (qg): a unified angular-probability unit and metric for parametric quantum circuit design."
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = ">=3.9"
license = { text = "MIT" }
authors = [
    { name = "Vicente Humberto Monteverde", email = "viny01958@gmail.com" }
]
classifiers = [
    "Programming Language :: Python :: 3",
    "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License",
    "Operating System :: OS Independent",
    "Topic :: Scientific/Engineering :: Physics",
    "Intended Audience :: Science/Research",
]
dependencies = [
    "numpy>=1.24",
]

[project.optional-dependencies]
qiskit = ["qiskit>=1.0", "qiskit-aer"]
cirq = ["cirq>=1.0"]
dev = ["pytest>=7.0", "matplotlib>=3.7"]
all = ["qiskit>=1.0", "qiskit-aer", "cirq>=1.0", "pytest>=7.0", "matplotlib>=3.7"]

[project.urls]
Homepage = "https://github.com/Viny2030/quang"
Repository = "https://github.com/Viny2030/quang.git"
"Bug Tracker" = "https://github.com/Viny2030/quang/issues"
Paper = "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22832150"

[tool.setuptools.packages.find]
include = ["quang*"]
3. Archivo Nuevo OBLIGATORIO para JOSS: CONTRIBUTING.md
JOSS exige explícitamente que los proyectos demuestren cómo otros investigadores pueden colaborar o reportar bugs. Crea este archivo en la raíz del repositorio:

Markdown
# Contributing to quang

Thank you for your interest in contributing to `quang`! We welcome bug reports, documentation improvements, algorithmic extensions, and feature requests.

## Reporting Issues

If you encounter a bug or unexpected behavior:
1. Search the [existing issues](https://github.com/Viny2030/quang/issues) to ensure it has not already been reported.
2. If it is new, open an issue providing:
   - A clear description of the problem.
   - A minimal, reproducible code example.
   - Your Python and library versions (`quang`, `numpy`, `qiskit`, `cirq`).

## Submitting Pull Requests

1. Fork the repository and create a feature branch:
   ```bash
   git checkout -b feature/my-new-feature
Install the package in editable mode with development dependencies:

Bash
pip install -e ".[all]"
Implement your changes and write accompanying tests in tests/.

Ensure all tests pass:

Bash
pytest
Commit your changes with descriptive commit messages and submit a Pull Request against the main branch.


